iOS native views: the app's UIView shown beneath GPUI's non-opaque CAMetalLayer, through gpui_apple's hole (needs 0048 and 0051).

What:
- `IosWindow::attach_native_view` puts the view in a clipping container inserted directly below GPUI's view; `draw`
  plans each scene's placements (gpui_apple's shared `native_view_placement`, 0051), presents with
  `draw_placing_native_views` and then applies them without UIKit animation (frames, hidden, z-order, and
  `NativeView::set_background`'s colour on the host view behind them), as macOS's native_views.rs does. The code is in
  the new ios/window/native_views.rs; window.rs only hooks it in. A view is hidden in a scene that doesn't paint it
  and removed when its last handle drops.
- `native_view_input` is `Window`: GPUI's view stays on top and takes every touch, so pointer and scroll over a view
  reach its element's listeners; the view gets none unless the app forwards it.
- Rotation: GPUI's view and the containers live in a host view inside the view controller's plain root view. In
  `handle_layout_change` (inside UIKit's rotation animation) the host takes the new size without animation and only
  its centre follows the turn, and the frame at the new size is drawn right there, in the same transaction, so GPUI's
  content, the holes and the views turn as one rigid picture instead of GPUI's last frame stretching while the views
  jump. `viewWillTransitionToSize:withTransitionCoordinator:` marks the turn (`Rotation::Turning` until the
  coordinator's completion), during which the holes are cut a pixel inside the views (0051's `hole_inset`), since
  Core Animation resamples the turning edges; the frame after the turn cuts them exactly again. Display Zoom
  (`nativeScale` != `scale`) gets the same inset.
- Keyboard: `set_focused_native_view` gives keyboard input to a native view (`KeyboardInput::NativeView`): GPUI's
  text input view resigns and never becomes first responder, so the app can make its own UIView (a UITextField, a
  WKWebView) first responder. When GPUI focus comes back, a focused GPUI field takes first responder again, even
  though UIKit cleared its keyboard wish when the app's view took first responder from it.

Why: upstream's `NativeView::new` fails on iOS. Lifecycle, clipping and z-order follow Flutter's
FlutterPlatformViewsController.mm (oracle only: Flutter draws over its views, this draws under them; its touch
interceptor is the model for an app that forwards touches into its view). Flutter's FlutterViewController.mm
(`viewWillTransitionToSize:withTransitionCoordinator:`) only delays its viewport update to halfway through a turn,
which halves the stretch; rendering the new size at once keeps the views in step instead. The layout code is
gpui_macos's window/native_views.rs (upstream GPUI, Apache-2.0) adapted to UIKit, which is most of this patch's size.

Checked on the iOS 26.5 simulator (iPhone 17 at 3x, iPad Air 11-inch at 2x) with examples/native-view-apple: 0 GPUI
frames/s while the view recolours at 63 frames/s; slot-relative mouse downs and scrolls from taps and swipes; the div
over the view takes its own taps; 0 gap frames while the slot moves every frame (228 frames) and through rotations to
landscape and back (436, 360 and 615 frames); exact edges at 2x and 3x; typing goes to a native UITextField while its
handle is focused and back to GPUI's field after.

Upstream PR: not filed (filing paused, patch rule 14).

